    {
      "id": "r35",
      "num": "35",
      "zone": "z-longer-tours",
      "name": "Gray's Peak",
      "time": "8 - 10 hrs",
      "gain_m": 800,
      "descent_m": null,
      "description": "Gray's Peak is the ultimate big objective from Kokanee Glacier Cabin as takes a full day and lots of energy to complete as this is one of the highest peaks in the park. You will traverse Kokanee Glacier from its North end to to its South and get views of near-by Nelson from the 2,753 metre summit. You will cover approximately 22 kilometres round trip and reach the Southern boundary of the park crossing significant glaciated terrain so prepare accordingly and ensure you have enough skill, good weather and solid conditions. To reach the summit of Gray's Peak follow the directions to The Keyhole found above but continue on past Cond Peak (2,801) metres) and Kokanee Peak (2,790 metres). Once past Kokanee Peak you will get your first glimpse of Gray's Peak in the distance to the far South. After a brief descent of a few hundred metres you will end up at the saddle of Kokanee Peak and Gray's Peak, mark this spot as this is where you will descend heading West once you summit Gray's Peak itself. From here continue climbing another 220 metre to gain the summit of Gray's Peak. The last 15 metres is very steep and slightly exposed so you may want to boot pack this portions if conditions permit. The route down is by skiing back to the saddle we spoke of earlier. Ski down the West face contouring North as you go. Be careful not to ski lower than 2,000 meter elevation as you need to gain Kokanee Lake at this elevation. The terrain is very benchy and rolling so the ski tour back home is not as straight forward as you may think. This Tour will take a full day so leave early and be very well prepared.",
      "notes": "Guide note: Refers to The Keyhole directions, which are missing (see r34).\n\nPlacement: skin reuses r34's skin coordinates from the cabin onto Kokanee Glacier (to ~350 m short of the Keyhole), then a least-cost DEM path SE up the glacier past the NE sides of Cond Peak and Kokanee Peak (~2750 m), down ~210 m to the Kokanee Peak - Gray's Peak saddle (DEM 2539 m, exactly 220 m below the 2759 m lidar summit, as the guide says) and up the ridge to the summit; the last ~15 m is steep (boot pack, as stated). Descent goes back to the saddle, then down the West face contouring NW above 2000 m to the S end of Kokanee Lake (same end point as r34's descent; ~790 m vertical); two short cliff bands on the face were routed around on the 2 m grid. The return from Kokanee Lake to the cabin (N past Keen Lake) is not drawn. The descent and the new skin section are on lidar, but the skin as a whole is ~62% 30 m MRDEM (the shared approach), and the approach itself is inferred because the Keyhole directions are missing, so confidence is medium.",
      "approx": true,
      "confidence": "medium",
